A||||14||Oreif||How do these look?||||12||
Z||000000||Oreif||03-28-2000||12:09 PM||||I pulled the emblems off of my door. (they hide the screw) Filled in the embossed areas with an Epoxy ink (won't yellow) Then coated it with a urethane coating (also won't Yellow) What does everyone think?<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/emblem1.jpg"> <P>I did make 2 sets so I have an extra set.||||reg||12
Z||000001||Greg Piet||03-28-2000||12:12 PM||||I Like it!||||reg||1
Z||000002||87ECO5||03-28-2000||01:33 PM||||That DOES look good.  Nice job.||||reg||1
Z||000003||mrfixit58||03-28-2000||03:10 PM||||They look GREAT! What kind of epoxy paint (i.e., brand name) did you use?<P>Roy  ||||reg||14
Z||000004||fiero56||03-28-2000||03:31 PM||||I have always wanted to do that, but never thought I could without a crappy looking outcome. How exaclty did you do it?<P>Looks great!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Oreif||03-28-2000||10:22 PM||||The Epoxy Ink is by Hysol. It's a 2-part mix. I then cleaned them up with an old toothbrush and water. I applied the epoxy ink with a toothpick and let them dry overnight. Then I wet sanded the surface (black part) with 500 grit sand paper (gives you the very clean edges) Then sprayed a thin coat of Wheel Urethane paint that I bought at trak auto. (suppose to be for aluminum rims) I did 2 sets (in case i screwed one up) and both sets came out perfect! So I have an extra set.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Bill Osmer||03-29-2000||10:05 AM||||Is there any reason one couldn't use white appliance epoxy paint?? It's very hard when cured, and is VERY available from hardware stores. Also, if it's epoxy, why do you need a clear over coat?||||reg||5
Z||000007||Oreif||03-29-2000||11:12 AM||||You can use any kind of white epoxy. White paint has a tendancy to yellow over time. The reason I used a clear coat is because I wet sanded the surface to remove scratches and excess epoxy. This is what makes the edges sharp. After wet sanding the black is dull. The clear coat gives it a high gloss shine and protects it.||||reg||1
Z||000008||fiero56||03-29-2000||01:52 PM||||Now I get it. I can get white epoxy all over the thing if I want, I can just sand it off when finished. Why didn't I think of that. Guess I know what I'll be doing soon.||||reg||1
Z||000009||TheFiero64||03-29-2000||03:37 PM||||I did the same thing but with gold model paint. The gold goes good with my saddle int.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Jan02^07.jpg">   <BR>It also goes good with my other custom mod. I took the cover from an 86 owners manual and put it in the panel where the power window switches go. looks like leather. take a look.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Jan02^06.jpg">   <BR>My only prob. is how to do this on my GT because it has power mirrors!<p>[This message has been edited by TheFiero64 (edited 03-29-2000).]||||reg||10
Z||000010||Oreif||03-31-2000||02:48 AM||||The Gold looks good! I tried white model paint but it yellowed after a few months. If any one has a yellow 88 I have a set!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
